1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,399
Your private endpoints don't sit in a dedicated network.

2
00:00:03,449 --> 00:00:07,162
Each spoke keeps its own, right next to the workloads that use them.

3
00:00:07,212 --> 00:00:11,533
And the requirement is that every connection to them goes through the Palo Alto

4
00:00:11,583 --> 00:00:12,640
firewall in the hub.

5
00:00:12,690 --> 00:00:17,620
If that's your setup and the traffic keeps skipping the firewall, this lecture is for you.

6
00:00:18,570 --> 00:00:23,559
We'll see exactly why the traffic bypasses the firewall, the one routing rule that

7
00:00:23,609 --> 00:00:28,843
fixes it, how to keep the return path symmetric, the PAN-OS configuration, and finally

8
00:00:28,893 --> 00:00:32,960
a Terraform lab you can deploy to prove each piece with real tests.

9
00:00:33,910 --> 00:00:35,706
Here's the topology.

10
00:00:35,756 --> 00:00:43,646
The hub, 10.0.0.0/16, has a VM-Series firewall in the trust subnet at 10.0.2.4, behind

11
00:00:43,696 --> 00:00:48,078
an internal load balancer with an HA ports rule.

12
00:00:48,127 --> 00:00:54,540
Its frontend, 10.0.2.100, is the next hop for every route we'll write.

13
00:00:55,490 --> 00:00:57,495
Two spokes are peered to the hub.

14
00:00:57,545 --> 00:01:01,604
Each spoke has an app subnet with a test VM, and a separate subnet

15
00:01:01,654 --> 00:01:03,285
just for private endpoints.

16
00:01:03,335 --> 00:01:07,021
Spoke 1 has endpoints for a storage account and a key vault.

17
00:01:07,071 --> 00:01:09,760
Spoke 2 has one for its own storage account.

18
00:01:10,710 --> 00:01:14,827
Now look at vm1 talking to the blob endpoint in its own spoke.

19
00:01:14,877 --> 00:01:18,659
Its effective routes show why the firewall never sees it.

20
00:01:18,709 --> 00:01:23,095
The endpoint created a slash 32 route, next hop InterfaceEndpoint.

21
00:01:23,145 --> 00:01:25,918
Everything else in the spoke is VnetLocal.

22
00:01:25,968 --> 00:01:31,900
Your default route to the firewall loses to both, because the longest prefix always wins.

23
00:01:32,850 --> 00:01:35,060
So there are two separate causes.

24
00:01:35,110 --> 00:01:41,429
Inside a VNet, subnet-to-subnet traffic is delivered directly, with no appliance in the path.

25
00:01:41,479 --> 00:01:46,291
And each private endpoint injects a slash 32 into its own VNet and into

26
00:01:46,341 --> 00:01:47,524
every peered VNet.

27
00:01:47,574 --> 00:01:50,400
A default route can never beat a slash 32.

28
00:01:51,350 --> 00:01:53,847
Here's the rule that fixes it.

29
00:01:53,897 --> 00:02:01,488
With network policies turned on for the endpoint subnet, a user-defined route can override

30
00:02:01,538 --> 00:02:07,176
the endpoint's slash 32, but only if its prefix is at least as long

31
00:02:07,226 --> 00:02:12,100
as the address space of the VNet where the endpoint lives.

32
00:02:12,150 --> 00:02:13,967
Spoke 1 is a slash 16.

33
00:02:14,017 --> 00:02:16,175
So 0.0.0.0/0 doesn't work.

34
00:02:16,225 --> 00:02:22,627
10.0.0.0/8 doesn't work either, and that's the one that catches most people.

35
00:02:22,677 --> 00:02:28,060
10.1.0.0/16 works, and so does the endpoint subnet, 10.1.2.0/24.

36
00:02:29,010 --> 00:02:35,150
The switch is a property called privateEndpointNetworkPolicies, and it goes on the subnet that

37
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:38,178
hosts the endpoints, not on the client subnet.

38
00:02:38,228 --> 00:02:41,602
RouteTableEnabled lets routes override the slash 32.

39
00:02:41,652 --> 00:02:45,685
NetworkSecurityGroupEnabled makes NSGs apply to the endpoints.

40
00:02:45,735 --> 00:02:47,660
Set it to Enabled to get both.

41
00:02:47,710 --> 00:02:50,360
In Terraform it's one line on the subnet.

42
00:02:51,310 --> 00:02:53,811
The endpoint subnet route is the sweet spot.

43
00:02:53,861 --> 00:02:57,812
It's one route per spoke instead of one per endpoint, so you stay far

44
00:02:57,862 --> 00:02:59,320
below the 400 route limit.

45
00:02:59,370 --> 00:03:04,364
App-to-app traffic inside the spoke stays local, and any new endpoint dropped into that

46
00:03:04,414 --> 00:03:06,220
subnet is covered automatically.

47
00:03:07,170 --> 00:03:11,760
There are three flows to cover, and each one needs its route in a different place.

48
00:03:12,710 --> 00:03:16,690
Flow one, a VM reaching an endpoint in its own spoke.

49
00:03:16,740 --> 00:03:21,632
Flow two, a VM in spoke 2 reaching the vault endpoint in spoke 1.

50
00:03:21,682 --> 00:03:29,540
Flow three, on-premises clients reaching an endpoint in spoke 2 through the ExpressRoute or VPN gateway.

51
00:03:30,490 --> 00:03:34,290
Same spoke needs the endpoint subnet route on the app subnet.

52
00:03:34,340 --> 00:03:38,204
Cross spoke needs the remote spoke's prefix on the app subnet.

53
00:03:38,254 --> 00:03:43,506
On-premises needs the spoke prefixes on a route table attached to the GatewaySubnet.

54
00:03:43,556 --> 00:03:45,400
Let's take them one at a time.

55
00:03:46,350 --> 00:03:47,252
Flow one.

56
00:03:47,302 --> 00:03:56,137
On spoke 1's app subnet, the route table rt-spoke1-app gets a route for 10.1.2.0/24,

57
00:03:56,187 --> 00:04:01,003
the endpoint subnet, with next hop 10.0.2.100.

58
00:04:01,053 --> 00:04:05,340
Turn off gateway route propagation on it.

59
00:04:06,290 --> 00:04:11,266
Now vm1's packet to the blob endpoint goes across the peering to the load

60
00:04:11,316 --> 00:04:16,085
balancer, then to the firewall, and from the firewall to the endpoint.

61
00:04:16,135 --> 00:04:20,560
The same table also carries the route to spoke 2 and, if you want

62
00:04:20,610 --> 00:04:23,520
internet egress inspected, a default route.

63
00:04:24,470 --> 00:04:27,416
Check the effective routes on vm1 again.

64
00:04:27,466 --> 00:04:33,708
The endpoint's slash 32 now shows as Invalid, and your slash 24 is the active route.

65
00:04:33,758 --> 00:04:38,802
The VNet's own slash 16 is still VnetLocal, so the app tiers talk to

66
00:04:38,852 --> 00:04:40,300
each other directly.

67
00:04:41,250 --> 00:04:44,547
Getting the packet to the firewall is half the job.

68
00:04:44,597 --> 00:04:47,894
The endpoint has to reply through the firewall too.

69
00:04:47,944 --> 00:04:54,062
Without help, the endpoint sees vm1's real address and replies straight back inside the spoke.

70
00:04:54,113 --> 00:04:58,000
The firewall sees half a conversation and drops the session.

71
00:04:58,950 --> 00:05:00,221
There are two fixes.

72
00:05:00,271 --> 00:05:05,375
One is source NAT on the firewall toward the endpoint subnets, so the endpoint

73
00:05:05,425 --> 00:05:08,018
replies to the firewall's trust address.

74
00:05:08,068 --> 00:05:11,982
The other is the disableSnatOnPL tag on the firewall's NICs.

75
00:05:12,032 --> 00:05:17,532
The platform then returns endpoint traffic to the firewall and keeps the real client

76
00:05:17,582 --> 00:05:19,580
IP, which is nicer for logging.

77
00:05:20,530 --> 00:05:22,873
Watch the NSG on the endpoint subnet.

78
00:05:22,923 --> 00:05:26,689
It matches the source address that arrives at the endpoint.

79
00:05:26,739 --> 00:05:30,117
Allow only the firewall works only when you use SNAT.

80
00:05:30,167 --> 00:05:34,839
Without SNAT, the NSG can't tell a firewalled packet from a bypass, so it

81
00:05:34,889 --> 00:05:39,560
has to allow the client subnets, and the route becomes your real control.

82
00:05:40,510 --> 00:05:42,340
Flow two, cross spoke.

83
00:05:42,390 --> 00:05:47,810
In a plain hub and spoke, the spokes are peered only to the hub,

84
00:05:47,860 --> 00:05:52,597
so spoke 2 has no route to spoke 1 at all except the one

85
00:05:52,647 --> 00:05:53,622
you give it.

86
00:05:53,672 --> 00:06:02,340
rt-spoke2-app sends 10.1.0.0/16 to the load balancer, and the firewall forwards to the vault endpoint.

87
00:06:03,290 --> 00:06:08,475
The hub learns spoke 1's prefix and every endpoint slash 32 over its peering,

88
00:06:08,525 --> 00:06:11,806
so the firewall can reach the endpoints directly.

89
00:06:11,856 --> 00:06:17,041
Add the mirror route on spoke 1, 10.2.0.0/16 to the load balancer, so traffic

90
00:06:17,091 --> 00:06:19,760
in the other direction is inspected too.

91
00:06:20,710 --> 00:06:21,424
One warning.

92
00:06:21,474 --> 00:06:26,324
If spokes are peered directly, or joined in a Virtual Network Manager mesh or

93
00:06:26,374 --> 00:06:31,033
connected group, every endpoint slash 32 propagates into the other spokes.

94
00:06:31,083 --> 00:06:32,433
The same rule applies.

95
00:06:32,483 --> 00:06:36,824
The route must be at least as long as the remote spoke's VNet prefix,

96
00:06:36,874 --> 00:06:38,860
and network policies must be on.

97
00:06:39,810 --> 00:06:41,279
Flow three, on-premises.

98
00:06:41,329 --> 00:06:44,570
Traffic arrives at the gateway in the GatewaySubnet.

99
00:06:44,620 --> 00:06:50,202
The gateway's effective routes include each spoke prefix and every endpoint slash 32, all

100
00:06:50,252 --> 00:06:51,531
learned over peering.

101
00:06:51,581 --> 00:06:57,480
So the GatewaySubnet gets its own route table, rt-gateway, that sends traffic to the firewall.

102
00:06:58,430 --> 00:07:06,164
Its routes are the spoke address spaces, 10.1.0.0/16 and 10.2.0.0/16, to the load balancer.

103
00:07:06,214 --> 00:07:13,264
A single 10.0.0.0/8 route is too short, and the endpoint slash 32s would still win.

104
00:07:13,314 --> 00:07:20,620
Keep gateway route propagation on, and never put a default route on the GatewaySubnet.

105
00:07:21,570 --> 00:07:22,574
Two more pieces.

106
00:07:22,624 --> 00:07:27,778
The spokes peer with use remote gateways, so on-premises learns their prefixes.

107
00:07:27,828 --> 00:07:34,892
And on-premises DNS forwards the public zones, blob.core.windows.net and vault.azure.net, to the DNS Private

108
00:07:34,942 --> 00:07:39,240
Resolver's inbound IP, so names resolve to the endpoint addresses.

109
00:07:40,190 --> 00:07:44,986
On the firewall, keep it one-armed on the trust interface.

110
00:07:45,036 --> 00:07:48,914
Ethernet 1/1 is untrust, ethernet 1/2 is trust.

111
00:07:48,964 --> 00:07:55,682
A static route sends 10.0.0.0/8 out ethernet 1/2 to the subnet gateway, 10.0.2.1.

112
00:07:55,732 --> 00:08:03,035
A host route and a management profile answer the load balancer probe from 168.63.129.16.

113
00:08:03,085 --> 00:08:09,469
Then add one security rule from the app subnets to the endpoint subnets, plus

114
00:08:09,519 --> 00:08:11,140
the source NAT rule.

115
00:08:12,090 --> 00:08:17,227
Because traffic enters and leaves on ethernet 1/2, the firewall sees it as trust

116
00:08:17,277 --> 00:08:19,108
to trust, which is intrazone.

117
00:08:19,158 --> 00:08:24,944
PAN-OS allows intrazone traffic by default, so override intrazone-default to deny and log.

118
00:08:24,994 --> 00:08:29,029
Otherwise any spoke reaches any endpoint and nothing is logged.

119
00:08:29,079 --> 00:08:34,800
With two firewalls, the HA ports rule keeps each flow on one firewall in both directions.

120
00:08:35,750 --> 00:08:37,762
Now lock it down so it can't drift.

121
00:08:37,812 --> 00:08:39,883
Put an NSG on every endpoint subnet.

122
00:08:39,933 --> 00:08:43,831
Use Azure Policy to deny endpoint subnets without network policies.

123
00:08:43,881 --> 00:08:46,365
And don't forget App Service and Functions.

124
00:08:46,415 --> 00:08:50,725
Their VNet integration subnet is a client subnet too, so it needs the same

125
00:08:50,775 --> 00:08:54,260
route table, with all outbound traffic routed into the VNet.

126
00:08:55,210 --> 00:08:59,177
DNS doesn't change the path, but names must resolve privately.

127
00:08:59,227 --> 00:09:03,517
Keep the privatelink zones in one place, link them to the hub or to

128
00:09:03,567 --> 00:09:08,506
every spoke, and point the firewall's own DNS at the resolver so FQDN objects

129
00:09:08,556 --> 00:09:10,320
match the private addresses.

130
00:09:11,270 --> 00:09:14,938
And if you can ever move endpoints, there are other layouts.

131
00:09:14,988 --> 00:09:20,454
A dedicated endpoint spoke needs just one route and is Microsoft's most scalable pattern.

132
00:09:20,504 --> 00:09:25,845
Virtual WAN with routing intent attracts private traffic to a supported firewall in the

133
00:09:25,895 --> 00:09:28,820
virtual hub, with no routes to maintain by hand.

134
00:09:29,770 --> 00:09:30,957
Now let's prove it.

135
00:09:31,007 --> 00:09:34,016
The Terraform lab builds exactly this topology:

136
00:09:34,066 --> 00:09:39,028
the hub with a VM-Series and the internal load balancer, two spokes with test

137
00:09:39,078 --> 00:09:44,496
VMs, two storage accounts and a key vault with public access disabled, their private

138
00:09:44,546 --> 00:09:47,360
endpoints, DNS zones, route tables and NSGs.

139
00:09:48,310 --> 00:09:50,578
main.tf builds the Azure side.

140
00:09:50,628 --> 00:09:57,068
panos/fw-config.set is the firewall configuration you paste into the CLI and commit.

141
00:09:57,118 --> 00:10:02,940
tests/run-tests.sh runs curl from both VMs and shows their effective routes.

142
00:10:03,890 --> 00:10:07,112
Three variables turn it into an experiment.

143
00:10:07,162 --> 00:10:12,135
steer_through_firewall adds or removes the endpoint subnet routes.

144
00:10:12,185 --> 00:10:17,386
snat_mode switches between firewall SNAT and the disableSnatOnPL tag.

145
00:10:17,436 --> 00:10:21,800
And lock_pe_subnets_with_nsg adds the endpoint subnet NSG.

146
00:10:22,750 --> 00:10:24,213
Here's what to expect.

147
00:10:24,263 --> 00:10:28,750
With steering on, SNAT and the NSG, both VMs get an HTTP 4xx back.

148
00:10:28,800 --> 00:10:33,769
That means TLS reached the endpoint, and the firewall shows each session.

149
00:10:33,819 --> 00:10:38,238
Turn steering off with the NSG still on, and vm1 times out to its

150
00:10:38,288 --> 00:10:43,051
own endpoints, because the NSG drops a source that isn't the firewall.

151
00:10:43,101 --> 00:10:46,420
Turn the NSG off as well, and you see the bypass.

152
00:10:47,370 --> 00:10:49,826
If it doesn't work, check in this order.

153
00:10:49,876 --> 00:10:55,151
Network policies on the endpoint subnet, the route prefix length, and the route table

154
00:10:55,201 --> 00:10:56,404
on the right subnet.

155
00:10:56,454 --> 00:11:01,417
A session that times out after the firewall means the return path is asymmetric.

156
00:11:01,467 --> 00:11:04,925
If names resolve to a public IP, a zone link is missing.

157
00:11:04,975 --> 00:11:09,749
Destroy the lab when you're done, because the firewall is billed by the hour.

158
00:11:09,799 --> 00:11:13,947
The diagrams, script and Terraform are in the demo-labs repository.

159
00:11:13,997 --> 00:11:15,200
Thanks for watching.
